<title>Exploring the derivative of the exponential function</title>
<p><ref>Video</ref> | <ref>Applets</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref>  </p>

<section><title>Transcript: The derivative of an exponential function</title>

<p>Let <m>f(x)</m> be an exponential function. Let’s say <m>f(x) = 2^x</m>. To calculate the derivative <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>, we need to use the limit definition.</p>
  
<p>So this is the limit as <m>h</m> goes to zero of <m>f(x + h)- f(x)</m>, all over <m>h</m>
<me>\lim_{h \to 0}\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}.</me>
Plugging in the value for <m>f</m>, <m>f(x+h)</m> is <m>2^{(x + h)}</m> minus <m>f(x)</m>, <m>2^x</m>, all over <m>h</m>. To simplify this expression we can use the rules for exponents. We know that <m>2^{(x+h)}</m> is the same thing as <m>2^x</m> times <m>2^h</m>, so this limit becomes <m>2^x * 2^h - 2^x</m>, all over <m>h</m>. We can factor out the <m>2^x</m>; in fact we can even take the <m>2^x</m> out of the limit, since it doesn’t depend on <m>h</m>
<me> 2^x \cdot \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{2^{(h - 1)}}{h}</me>
Now this looks ugly still but this expression here is just a number; it doesn’t depend on <m>x</m> at all. What is this number? You can use the applet below to get a numerical estimate of this quantity. But notice something else, what if I took <m>\frac{df}{dx}</m>, and set <m>x = 0</m>. Going back to this form for the derivative, we get that this is the limit as <m>h</m> goes to 0 of <m>2^{(0+h)} - 2^0</m> , all over <m>h</m>, and this quantity is exactly the same as what we have over here. So therefore the derivative of <m>2^x</m>  is equal to the derivative at 0 times <m>2^x</m>, or we could write it as <m>f’(x)</m> is <m>f’(0)</m> times the function itself, <m>f(x)</m>, which is <m>2^x</m>. So that's interesting. The derivative of <m>f</m> is just proportional to the function itself - with this strange proportionality constant, which is the same as <m>f’(0)</m>. Using the second applet below you can explore this.</p> <!--(2:48)-->
<p>Here’s what this applet looks like: the function <m>f(x) = 2^x</m> is plotted by the blue curve, and its derivative is plotted by the green curve. According to the formula we just derived, the derivative should be proportional to the function itself. Here we take the ratio between the derivative and the function, evaluated at 1 because its the point we’re on, and we can see we get about 0.69, and if the green derivative curve is really proportional to the blue function curve, this ratio should stay the same for all values of <m>x</m>. And you can watch this ratio right here, the function values themselves change a lot, but the ratio stays more or less the same at 0.69. And this ratio is that funny looking expression we saw. Supposedly the ratio is the value of the derivative at 0, so if we plug in <m>x = 0</m> or <m>x_0 = 0</m>, the derivative itself is indeed about 0.69, the same value as the ratio between the derivative and the function, because <m>f(0)</m> is 1.</p>
<p>What about for other bases besides 2? What about the function <m>g(x) = b^x</m>, for some base <m>b</m> positive? If you repeat the same calculation we did before, only using <m>b</m> rather than 2, you would get that the derivative, <m>g’(x)</m> ends up being the same thing we had before. The derivative ends up being proportional to the function <m>g</m>, with proportionality constant the derivative at 0, <m>g’(0)</m>. And what does <m>g’(0)</m> end up being? Just like we had for the case when <m>b</m> was equal to 2, it’s this funny limit, where we have the parameter <m>b</m> in place of the number 2. 
<me> b^x \cdot \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{b^{(h - 1)}}{h}</me>
Well that's kind of annoying, we get this ugly number, and we get a different ugly number for each base <m>b</m> that we choose. Well, why not choose a base <m>b</m> where this ugly expression ends up being something nice? Can we find a base <m>b</m> where the ugly expression <m>g’(0)</m> isn’t so ugly? In fact why don’t we make it the easiest factor in the world, let’s make it the number 1.</p>
<p>Well, it turns out we can do that, we can find a number <m>b</m> for which this is true. And we’ll define the number, call it <m>e</m> as the value of <m>b</m> where this is true. Its the value where the derivative at 0 is just 1. If we use this special number, then the derivative is very simple. Then, <m>g’(x)</m>, well its just 1 times <m>g</m>, it's just <m>g</m> itself. In other words, <m>\frac{d}{dx}</m>, the derivative of <m>e^x</m> is equal to the function <m>e^x</m> itself. That’s pretty cool. That’s why we like the number <m>e</m> so well. Well, what is this value <m>e</m>, what does it look like? Well unfortunately e doesn’t have a nice value, it's some irrational number. Using the applets on this page, you can search for the value of <m>b</m> for which this is 1, and you’ll find that this value <m>b</m>, we’re calling it <m>e</m>, is approximately 2.71828. So the price we pay for having such a nice formula for the derivative is that our base is a weird number. But because this property is so nice, <m>e</m> is the most common base used for the exponential function in the sciences. And in fact we often call the function <m>e^x</m>, the exponential function. So we often say that the derivative of the exponential function is the function itself.</p> 
</section>
